            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Spanish for the berth, then the Spanish for to get off."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: la terminal",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la terminal — \"the terminal\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Los vuelos internacionales salen de la terminal dos. International flights leave from terminal two."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "At an airport it is feminine: la terminal; el terminal is a device."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "terminal is a learned borrowing from Latin terminālis \"of a boundary\" (EtymDB), from terminus \"a boundary, an end\" (Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
